@@ -0,0 +1,49 @@
+# Design
+
+## Context
+
+`POST /v1/executions/{id}/signal` flows through `run_idempotent_mutation_inner` (`apps/cli/src/commands/serve/mod.rs`). Before any command body runs, `classify_existing_keys` scans the execution's committed history and classifies the request's derived idempotency key(s):
+
+- `KeyState::Complete(sequence)` → the exact derived key exists and its event matches the full decision identity (actor, scope/stream, execution, and — for signals — payload fields including `envelope_sha256`, via `MutationDecisionKind::SignalRecorded.matches`). The request is answered by `reply_with_current_status`: the same `execution.status` projection every mutation can report honestly, plus the `idempotency` proof block inserted by `annotate_recognized_retry`.
+- `KeyState::Divergent` / `Partial` → 409 conflict, no proof. This is where a mismatched actor or a wrong-kind event under the same key already lands.
+
+The matched event is a `SignalRecorded` payload that carries the durable `signal_id` (`core/protocols/src/event.rs`). Classification currently keeps only the event's sequence — the id is dropped. The fresh-success path (`execution::signal::execute`) answers with `data.signalId` from the admitted record, so only the retry reply lacks it.
+
+## Goals / Non-Goals
+
+**Goals:**
+- A recognised retry of `execution.signal` carries the original durable `signalId`.
+- Refusal replies (mismatched actor, wrong-kind, divergent body) keep carrying no proof and no `signalId`.
+- All other mutation replies stay byte-identical.
+
+**Non-Goals:**
+- No change to classification semantics, conflict shapes, or the `idempotency` block's own fields.
+- No new durable identifiers for other mutations (e.g. approve/start); they have none to return.
+- No schema, core-crate, event-store, or CLI-envelope changes.
+
+## Decisions
+
+**1. Read the id back from the authenticated committed event, not from the request body.**
+Extraction happens in `classify_one_key`, on the exact-key + full-match path, and only when the command's decision kind is `SignalRecorded` (at which point `matches()` has already guaranteed the event kind and its `signal_id`). Alternatives: echo `request_body["signal"]["id"]` — equivalent in value for recognised retries (the match checks equality), but it makes the reply a claim about the request rather than about the store, asymmetric with `originalDecisionSequence`, which comes from the event. Or re-scan history for the signal event — needs a second match pass and risks picking up an unrelated signal of another actor.
+
+**2. Carry it as `Option` through the existing state, gated on decision kind.**
+`KeyPresence::Present(u64)` and `KeyState::Complete(u64)` gain the signal id (`Option<String>`; absent for every non-signal command). Both `Complete` call sites — the pre-flight classification and the post-append `GHE003` reclassification race — go through the same helpers, so the racing second attempt gets identical behaviour for free. The command string is not consulted: the decision kind is the single source of truth for "this command records a signal".
+
+**3. Insert at top-level `data.signalId`, mirroring the fresh-success shape.**
+The fresh reply already exposes `data.signalId`; the recognised retry then returns the same field name for the same value, so a client consumes both replies identically. Placing it inside the `idempotency` block was considered and rejected: existing tests equality-assert that block's exact shape, and the block's role is "this was a retry of committed work", not "here is the record's identity". `annotate_recognized_retry` gains the `Option` and inserts the field only when present.
+
+**4. Field order in the JSON is irrelevant to the contract; insertion into the existing status object is a one-line addition**, so no response-shape rework.
+
+## Risks / Trade-offs
+
+- [Additive wire field on one endpoint's retry success] → Non-breaking for tolerant parsers (the repository's forward-compatibility rule preserves unknown fields); the only strict-shape consumer found in-repo is this test suite, which is updated with the change.
+- [Extracting from the event assumes the matched event is a `SignalRecorded`] → Guaranteed: extraction is behind both the exact-key check and `MutationDecisionKind::SignalRecorded.matches`, and wrong-kind/mismatched-actor events classify as `Divergent` before any id is read.
+- [A future second signal-derived key could make "first decision sequence" ambiguous] → `keys` is a `Vec` today but every wired mutation supplies exactly one suffix; the option carries the id of the first matching decision, consistent with how `original_decision_sequence` is already chosen. If multi-decision signal routes ever exist, this field's definition must be revisited then.
+
+## Migration Plan
+
+No migration: additive-only. Rollback is reverting the change; stored events are untouched either way (the id was always on the wire).
+
+## Open Questions
+
+None.
